// Practice quiz: handling NULLs, COALESCE and CASE
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters and treats colon, equals, hash, tilde and braces as syntax, so those are escaped.

$CATEGORY: sql-jdbc-foundations/crud-aggregates-and-joins/handling-nulls-coalesce-and-case

::o1 equals NULL::Three members have no phone number recorded. How many rows does WHERE phone \= NULL return? {
=None#Correct. Comparing anything with NULL using \= gives unknown, and WHERE keeps only rows where the condition is true.
~Three#Not quite. Those three rows need phone IS NULL.
~An ERROR#Not quite. The comparison is legal; it is simply never true.
~Every row#Not quite. Unknown is not true, so no row passes.
}

::o1 present values::Which condition keeps only the members whose phone number is recorded? {
=phone IS NOT NULL#Correct. IS NOT NULL is true exactly when a value is present.
~phone !\= NULL#Not quite. Any comparison with NULL, including !\=, is unknown for every row, so WHERE keeps no members at all.
~NOT (phone \= NULL)#Not quite. phone \= NULL is unknown, and NOT unknown is still unknown, so no row passes.
~phone IS NULL#Not quite. That keeps the opposite rows\: the ones with no phone.
}

::o2 first non-NULL::For a row where nickname is NULL and first_name is 'Ana', what does COALESCE(nickname, first_name, 'Guest') return? {
='Ana'#Correct. COALESCE returns its first argument that is not NULL, reading left to right.
~'Guest'#Not quite. The fallback is used only if every earlier argument is NULL.
~NULL#Not quite. COALESCE returns NULL only when all of its arguments are NULL.
~'Ana Guest'#Not quite. COALESCE picks one value; it does not join them.
}

::o2 stored value::A report runs SELECT COALESCE(city, 'Unknown') FROM members;. What happens to the stored NULL cities? {
=Nothing\: only the query result shows Unknown#Correct. COALESCE in a SELECT changes the result, not the rows in the table.
~They are saved as 'Unknown'#Not quite. Changing stored values needs an UPDATE.
~They are deleted#Not quite. A SELECT never removes rows.
~They become empty strings#Not quite. The stored values stay NULL.
}

::o3 first true WHEN::For points \= 90, what does CASE WHEN points BETWEEN 50 AND 100 THEN 'Pass' WHEN points BETWEEN 80 AND 100 THEN 'Merit' ELSE 'Review' END return? {
='Pass'#Correct. CASE returns the result of the first WHEN that is true, so later branches are never reached. Put the narrower test first.
~'Merit'#Not quite. Merit is never reached for 90, because the first WHEN is already true.
~'Review'#Not quite. ELSE is used only when no WHEN is true.
~'Pass Merit'#Not quite. CASE returns exactly one result.
}

::o3 no ELSE::A CASE expression has no ELSE, and none of its WHEN conditions is true for a row. What does it return for that row? {
=NULL#Correct. Without ELSE, a CASE with no true branch yields NULL.
~An ERROR#Not quite. A missing ELSE is legal.
~The first THEN value#Not quite. A THEN value is returned only when its WHEN is true.
~An empty string#Not quite. The default result is NULL, not empty text.
}
